  "summary": "The EFF reports on a California State Auditor investigation into ALPR use by LAPD, Fresno PD, Sacramento County Sheriff, and Marin County Sheriff, finding inadequate policies, weak auditing, excessive retention, broad data sharing, and insufficient safeguards against misuse and federal immigration access. EFF calls for legislative reforms including hot list limits, banning private-vendor storage, and a statewide moratorium.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "California police and sheriffs are failing to protect the privacy of drivers on city streets, the California State Auditor's office determined after a seven-month investigation",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "A member of law enforcement could misuse ALPR images to stalk an individual or observe vehicles at particular locations and events, such as doctors' offices or clinics and political rallies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "the three agencies storing ALPR data in Vigilant's cloud\u2014Fresno, Marin, and Sacramento\u2014do not have sufficient data security safeguards in their contracts",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Sacramento County's Sheriff's Office alone shares with more than 1,000 entities.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "Place a statewide moratorium on government use of ALPRs, similar to the moratorium on mobile face recognition passed last session.",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    }
  ],
